ay 1993 until now what about  just from when you shot the fugitive  versus  now given everything that's happened in  the world and specifically in the  country  over the last couple of months are you  now watching the finished product with  different themes and ideas taking i  don't know having even more value to  them than when you actually shot the  thing  well it's you know i mean the the world  has changed dynamically  uh you're dealing with a worldwide  pandemic you're dealing with a whole  social movement  uh black lives matter and more  uh across the nation uh you're having a  federal government  dictate policy that's never been  dictated in this country's history  uh you know so all it makes you think of  as well  be an interesting second second series  to make you know  uh if if as artists you draw upon  what is happening in the world around  you i would have to expect that  most artists around the world whether  they're painters writers actors or  musicians  uh are are having visceral reactions  to to what is happening some positive  some negative but  but visceral nonetheless i did want to  go back to  the 93 movie because i know you're  playing a different character  here kiefer but you are following up an  oscar-winning performance from  tommy lee jones it's one of my favorite  performances of his  even though it's a different character  and you got to make him your own  is there anything that he did in that  movie that you used as a source of  inspiration for your version of the  character well there were two things  that he did  uh and and part of it was the script and  and part of it was the way he  interpreted it but he was incredibly  funny  he he was so sarcastic uh  and sarcasm generally is the kind of  humor uh  that is embodied by someone who is very  cynical  uh and and both of those things  uh were integrated in our script as well  but i really wanted to lean into those  because people who have been chasing  criminals for 35 years  are cynical uh and that that's a real  danger because that's where they can  make their mistake that's where they can  just think they've seen it all  but that aspect of the tommy lee jones  character was something that  i just i could watch it today and and  enjoy it as much as i did the first time  i watched it in the theater  uh there are very few actors on the  planet  i believe that can do that that well um  so like boys said you don't try and fill  someone else's shoes you try and create  your own footprint  uh and hopefully an audience will enjoy  it  i've only been able to see four chapters  i don't want to spoil anything but i do  have to ask  is there anything akin to the i didn't  kill my wife i don't care conversation  in this version  not as direct but yes there there are  moments  where i'll have that conversation with  other people within my department uh  and and and and i'll leave it at that  and then maybe you'll be pleasantly  surprised down the road i'm looking  forward to finding those moments  um i know you had mentioned earlier that  you shot this  kind of like a movie but was there  anything over the course of the  production  process that challenged both of you in a  unique way with your craft and just  telling stories  i don't think there was any hindrance at  all working  on this project and sort of of how it's  given to the audience in these segments  it didn't affect anything i prepared for  this role as a  for a series or a film um  i think it really comes down to the  dynamic of of the editing  and the sort of evolution of the  platform of how they're being  sort of rolled out what about working  with nick santora on this because i've  covered a bunch of  uh quippy movies and shows and most of  the time when i talk to people it's  their first time experiencing a quibby  but  you guys had the luxury of working with  i mean i guess now  emmy nominated nick santora for uh most  dangerous games so  did you feel his influence kind of  helping you out with the format  well he's an incredible writer this  doesn't happen to me very often but  uh stephen hawkins had called me up and  we had done the first  season of 24 together and our great  friends and  uh and i think he's an extraordinary  director um  and he said well i've got this thing and  would you read it and i did i read it  and he said well what do you think and i  said  i'm in if you want to do it i'll i'd be  thrilled and he said well  call me back tomorrow and we'll go over  your notes  and i said i'll go have a drink with you  but i've got no notes  and he said what i said i love the  script i think it's fantastic  that doesn't happen very often you know  you might have a note because you don't  understand something or  something's not clear i had none and  then he said well i have a couple notes  and i said good don't change any of my  stuff though  and uh and he uh i just  that's such a rare thing to happen uh to  to find a piece of material that you  feel that strongly about i just think  he's an extraordinary writer  what was it like jumping back on set  with him i know it's been a while since  he directed you in 24 but was it kind of  just  you know picking up where you guys left  off in that sense i think you're so  focused on what you're doing at that  moment  you kind of stay focused on that um  but with stephen you know stephen has a  unique way of directing  uh that if you're halfway through a  scene and he's not engaged he really  will  yell out you're boring me and  there's very few people that i've worked  with that are confident enough to tell  you that in the middle  of shooting a sequence and it always  makes me laugh but then it always gets  me fired up to go back so  he he and i just have a nice way of  working together where  he makes you want to really reach down  deep and do your best  so what's an example of a moment where  he says you're boring me then what's his  follow-up what what's his uh  his note giving process to kind of